T13.01|Cost of equity is the return shareholders require for bearing an equity claim's risk. It is an opportunity cost used in valuation and capital decisions, not an expense that appears as a contractual interest payment. Market price and expected cash flows jointly imply a required return under a valuation model.~~CAPM, multifactor models and dividend-based estimates provide different approaches with assumptions and estimation uncertainty. A company's historical realised share return is not automatically its current cost of equity.|CAPM estimate: Risk-free rate plus compensation for systematic equity risk.;;Cash-flow-implied estimate: Rate consistent with price and forecast distributions.;;Build-up or multifactor estimate: Additional risk components under a stated model.|Assume risk-free rate 5%, equity risk premium 6% and beta 1.2.~~CAPM cost of equity = 5% + 1.2 × 6% = 12.2%.;;A ₹10 lakh one-year equity cash flow would have PV 10/1.122 ≈ ₹8.91.;;At beta 1.5, the model rate becomes 14%, reducing that PV.~~Equity requires compensation even without a contractual interest charge.~~The inputs are hypothetical estimates; model selection and currency consistency affect the result.|Identify the equity claim and currency.;;Estimate current required return rather than past performance.;;Report input uncertainty and cross-check models.
T13.02|CAPM relates expected excess return to exposure to market risk: Ke = Rf + beta × market risk premium. Beta measures systematic sensitivity under the model, while firm-specific risk is assumed diversifiable in the pricing framework.~~CAPM relies on restrictive assumptions and estimated inputs. It is a benchmark, not a guarantee that realised returns will equal the model prediction. A business with high standalone volatility can have a different beta than another equally volatile business.|Risk-free component: Compensation for the relevant time value.;;Market premium: Required compensation for market exposure.;;Beta component: Scale of exposure to the modelled market factor.|Use Rf 4%, market expected return 10% and beta 1.5.~~Market premium = 10% − 4% = 6%.;;Expected equity return = 4% + 1.5 × 6% = 13%.;;For beta 0.5, the model gives 7%, holding other assumptions fixed.~~Higher beta raises the required return in this model.~~The expected market return must be estimated for the purpose and currency; replacing it with one recent year's market return is unreliable.|Specify market and estimation horizon.;;Separate total from systematic risk.;;Test alternative beta and premium estimates.
T13.03|A risk-free rate should fit the currency and horizon of the cash flows and avoid unrecognised default exposure. The equity risk premium is expected or required market compensation above that baseline. Historical averages, surveys and implied estimates can differ substantially.~~A sovereign bond yield is not automatically risk-free when it includes meaningful default risk. Mixing a nominal local-currency rate with foreign-currency cash flows creates inconsistency; country risk and currency are related but distinct adjustments.|Historical premium: Based on past excess returns under stated conventions.;;Implied premium: Inferred from market price and cash-flow forecasts.;;Survey premium: Based on stated expectations or practice.|Assume a local government yield is 8%, including an estimated 2% sovereign default component, and an analyst uses a simplified default-adjustment approach.~~Adjusted baseline = 8% − 2% = 6%.;;With beta 1 and a 5% equity premium, model cost of equity is 11%.;;Using the unadjusted 8% and then adding the same default exposure again risks double counting.~~The components must be defined coherently.~~This decomposition is a modelling assumption, not a universal bond-yield subtraction rule.|Match currency and maturity.;;Identify default exposure in the baseline.;;Document premium method and date.
T13.04|Beta is often estimated by regressing a security's returns on market returns. It equals covariance with the market divided by market variance under the regression definition. The estimate depends on benchmark, observation frequency, lookback period and leverage during that history.~~A statistically estimated beta has uncertainty and may be distorted by illiquidity or structural change. Comparing several estimates and business-based peers can be more useful than accepting a single precise-looking number.|Raw regression beta: Estimate from historical return data.;;Adjusted beta: Estimate modified by a stated shrinkage convention.;;Fundamental or peer-based beta: Estimate informed by business exposures.|Stock-market covariance is 0.018 and market variance is 0.012 using compatible return units.~~Beta = 0.018/0.012 = 1.5.;;Interpret this as fitted market sensitivity, not a prediction of every daily move.;;If the company changes leverage materially, reassess whether the historical estimate fits the forecast.~~Beta 1.5 does not mean the stock always moves exactly 1.5 times the market.~~Regression residual risk remains, and estimates can change with data choices.|State benchmark and sampling window.;;Check liquidity and confidence intervals.;;Reconcile historical leverage with forecast leverage.
T13.05|Levered equity beta reflects operating exposure plus financial leverage. Unlevering aims to remove the financing effect so businesses with different debt mixes can be compared. A common simplified relationship is betaL = betaU[1 + (1 − tax rate)D/E], assuming negligible debt beta and other conditions.~~The tax adjustment and debt-beta assumption are not universal. Highly risky debt, changing leverage and differing tax-shield risk require richer treatment.|Levered beta: Equity sensitivity with financing effects.;;Unlevered beta: Estimated operating-asset sensitivity.;;Relevered beta: Operating beta translated to a target debt-equity mix.|Assume equity beta 1.5, debt/equity 0.5, tax rate 20% and negligible debt beta.~~Leverage factor = 1 + 0.8 × 0.5 = 1.4.;;Unlevered beta = 1.5/1.4 ≈ 1.0714.;;At target D/E 1, relevered beta ≈ 1.0714 × 1.8 = 1.9286.~~Higher target leverage increases modelled equity risk.~~Use consistent market-value ratios and assess whether the simplified assumptions remain reasonable.|State the beta formula assumptions.;;Use consistent debt and equity measures.;;Test debt risk and changing leverage.
T13.06|Bottom-up beta starts with comparable businesses, removes their financing effects, combines operating-risk estimates and applies the target company's financing structure. It is useful when the company's own return history is short or unreliable.~~Peer selection should reflect actual operating exposures and segment mix. Averaging many poorly matched firms does not solve comparability; weights should represent the economic composition being estimated.|Peer unlevering: Remove observed financing effects.;;Business mix weighting: Combine operating exposures by a stated value basis.;;Target relevering: Apply the forecast financing structure.|A company has 60% operating value in a segment with betaU 0.8 and 40% in one with betaU 1.2. Assume tax 20% and target D/E 0.5.~~Combined betaU = 0.6 × 0.8 + 0.4 × 1.2 = 0.96.;;Target leverage factor = 1 + 0.8 × 0.5 = 1.4.;;Equity beta = 0.96 × 1.4 = 1.344.~~Business composition and financing jointly determine the estimate.~~The value weights and peer betas are estimates; differing cash holdings and debt risk need consistent handling.|Select economically comparable peers.;;Separate operating assets from financing.;;Document segment weights and target leverage.
T13.07|Country risk can affect cash flows through political, legal, economic and transfer constraints. Currency consistency requires cash flows and discount rates to share a coherent denomination and inflation treatment. A high local-currency rate may partly reflect inflation rather than extra real business risk.~~Country-risk exposure can differ from incorporation location: customers, production, financing and contracts may span jurisdictions. Avoid adding a premium for a risk already reflected through probability-weighted cash-flow reductions.|Currency risk: Exposure to exchange-rate changes.;;Sovereign or transfer risk: Government payment or conversion restrictions.;;Operating country risk: Business exposure to local conditions.|Assume an equity model uses a 5% base rate, beta 1, global equity premium 6% and a separately justified 2% country component.~~Model rate = 5% + 6% + 2% = 13%.;;Check whether the forecast cash flows already include the same political-loss adjustment.;;If they do, reassess the additional premium to avoid overlapping adjustments.~~A coherent model treats each risk consistently.~~There is no universal country premium appropriate for every firm or every currency.|Map actual country exposures.;;Match cash and rate currency.;;Check risk overlap between cash forecasts and discount rates.
T13.08|Cost of debt is the current marginal required return on borrowing with relevant currency, maturity, seniority and credit risk. It is not necessarily the historical coupon paid on an old loan. Credit spreads compensate for default-related and other market exposures.~~After-tax cost is often approximated as Kd(1 − T) when interest deductions are usable and the assumptions fit. If deductions are limited or losses delay their use, a simple full tax benefit overstates value.|Pre-tax debt cost: Current required borrowing return.;;Credit spread: Increment above an appropriate benchmark.;;After-tax debt cost: Debt cost adjusted for an actually usable tax benefit.|A firm could borrow today at benchmark 6% plus 3% credit spread. Assume an immediately usable 20% interest tax benefit.~~Pre-tax debt cost = 9%.;;Simplified after-tax cost = 9% × (1 − 20%) = 7.2%.;;An old loan's 5% coupon does not establish a 5% cost of new capital.~~Historical payments and current marginal financing costs differ.~~Fees, amortisation, guarantees and deduction limits can change the effective cost.|Use current comparable borrowing terms.;;Match maturity, currency and priority.;;Assess actual tax-shield usability.
T13.09|Preferred capital and hybrids combine features of debt and equity. Preferred shares may have priority distributions or redemption terms; convertible instruments add conversion rights; subordinated or contingent instruments can absorb losses under specified conditions. Classification for accounting, law and valuation may differ.~~Value each contractual component and identify who receives cash under different outcomes. A fixed-looking preferred distribution may still be discretionary or deferrable, making its risk different from ordinary debt.|Preferred equity: Priority rights ahead of common equity.;;Convertible instrument: Funding claim plus conversion feature.;;Other hybrid: Contract combines financing and loss-absorption features.|A preferred share pays a perpetual annual ₹8 distribution and trades at ₹100. Assume fixed payments, no growth and the simplified perpetuity model.~~Implied preferred cost = 8/100 = 8%.;;At a required return of 10%, model value would be ₹80.;;Review payment discretion and redemption terms before applying the perpetuity model.~~The simple yield depends on the assumed contractual pattern.~~Conversion or redemption features require additional valuation rather than being ignored.|Read priority and payment rights.;;Separate embedded options.;;Distinguish accounting classification from economic risk.
T13.10|WACC combines required returns on the capital supporting operating cash flows, weighted by a consistent value-based financing mix. A basic formulation is E/(D + E)Ke + D/(D + E)Kd(1 − T). Additional capital classes require their own terms.~~WACC is appropriate only when cash-flow definition, risk, tax treatment and leverage assumptions fit. It is not a universal hurdle for every company project. Using FCFE with WACC mixes an equity cash flow with an all-capital rate.|Equity component: Market equity weight × cost of equity.;;Debt component: Debt weight × relevant after-tax debt cost.;;Additional capital: Preferred or hybrid components where applicable.|Assume equity weight 60%, debt weight 40%, Ke 12%, Kd 8% and usable tax benefit 25%.~~After-tax debt cost = 8% × 0.75 = 6%.;;Equity contribution = 60% × 12% = 7.2%.;;Debt contribution = 40% × 6% = 2.4%; WACC = 9.6%.~~The 9.6% rate belongs with compatible operating cash flows.~~If leverage or project risk changes, update the approach instead of mechanically retaining this rate.|Match WACC with FCFF or equivalent operating flows.;;Use coherent value weights.;;Test tax, leverage and project-risk assumptions.
T13.11|Market-value weights estimate how investors currently value financing claims. Book-value weights reflect accounting balances that may be historical and differ substantially from economic funding composition. A target structure may be used when it credibly represents future financing.~~The objective is a forward-looking opportunity cost, so current or justified target values usually matter more than original issuance amounts. Debt market values may be approximated by book values only when that approximation is reasonable.|Book-value weights: Accounting amounts.;;Market-value weights: Current economic claim values.;;Target weights: Explicit forecast financing policy.|Book equity is ₹40 lakh and debt ₹60, while market equity is ₹140 and debt value remains ₹60.~~Book debt weight = 60/100 = 60%.;;Market debt weight = 60/200 = 30%.;;Using Ke 12% and after-tax Kd 6%, market-weight WACC = 70% × 12% + 30% × 6% = 10.2%.~~Historical equity understates equity's current financing weight here.~~A large book-market difference can materially alter WACC; justify debt-value approximations.|Identify the valuation date.;;Estimate claim values consistently.;;Explain target structure and approximations.
T13.12|Historical financing cost describes what existing funding was originally priced to pay. Marginal cost describes the terms available for the next funding decision. Investment decisions require a current opportunity-cost assessment rather than assuming cheap old debt can finance unlimited new projects.~~As financing grows, spreads, dilution, covenants and funding availability may change. A marginal cost-of-capital schedule can therefore differ from a constant historical average.|Historical cost: Existing contractual financing terms.;;Marginal cost: Current next-unit funding opportunity.;;All-in cost: Yield plus relevant issuance and financing effects.|A company has old debt with 4% coupon but new comparable debt would require 9%. A new project is expected to earn 7% on compatible pre-tax debt-risk assumptions for illustration.~~The old coupon does not make new borrowing available at 4%.;;Compare the new decision with current relevant capital cost.;;Model any refinancing, issuance fees and funding constraints.~~Past financing terms cannot alone justify the new project.~~A complete corporate decision uses the project's total-capital risk and cash flows, not only a debt coupon comparison.|Use the cost available now.;;Assess funding scale and terms.;;Separate contractual payment forecasts from investment hurdle rates.
T13.13|Modigliani-Miller propositions isolate financing effects under explicit idealised assumptions. Without taxes, transaction frictions or distress costs, changing debt and equity does not change total firm value for unchanged operating assets. Equity becomes riskier as debt increases, offsetting the apparently cheaper debt component.~~Introducing corporate interest tax benefits changes the model. Real financing decisions additionally involve distress, agency, information and contracting effects, so the frictionless result is a benchmark rather than a literal description of every company.|No-tax benchmark: Financing mix does not create value under the ideal assumptions.;;Corporate-tax model: Debt can create a tax-shield benefit.;;Real-world extensions: Distress, incentives and frictions affect choices.|Assume an unlevered firm's operating assets are worth ₹100 lakh in the no-tax ideal model. It replaces ₹40 equity with debt.~~Operating asset cash flows are unchanged.;;Debt and equity values together remain ₹100 under the model.;;The remaining equity bears greater residual risk and requires a higher return.~~Relabelling claims does not create additional operating value in the frictionless benchmark.~~Tax shields or distress costs change the assumptions and therefore the conclusion.|State the model assumptions.;;Hold operating investment fixed when testing financing.;;Identify the real frictions relevant to the decision.
T13.14|An interest tax shield is the tax reduction arising from deductible interest under applicable conditions. Its timing and value depend on tax rules, taxable income, financing policy and risk. A simplified annual shield is interest × usable marginal tax rate.~~The present value is not always debt × tax rate; that shortcut requires particular permanent-debt and discounting assumptions. A forecast tax schedule is preferable when deductions are delayed or debt changes.|Current usable shield: Deduction reduces current cash tax.;;Deferred shield: Benefit may arise later subject to conditions.;;Unusable deduction: No modelled benefit when requirements are not met.|Interest is ₹10 lakh annually for two years and the assumed usable tax rate is 20%. Discount the shields at an illustrative 10%.~~Annual shield = ₹10 × 20% = ₹2.;;PV = 2/1.10 + 2/1.10² ≈ ₹3.47.;;If benefit is unavailable in year one, the forecast and value change.~~The shield is a dated cash-tax benefit, not simply an accounting label.~~Jurisdiction-specific deductibility and loss rules require current primary sources.|Model actual deduction availability.;;Date each cash-tax saving.;;Avoid counting the shield twice in WACC and APV.
T13.15|Trade-off theory balances debt benefits against expected distress and related costs. Pecking-order theory emphasises information asymmetry and a preference for internal funding before debt and then equity under its model. They explain different financing pressures rather than prescribing one universal debt ratio.~~Observed financing also reflects flexibility, asset collateral, ownership preferences and market access. A profitable firm may use little debt because it generates internal funds, even if debt has tax advantages.|Trade-off theory: Balance financing benefits and costs.;;Pecking-order theory: Funding hierarchy influenced by information costs.;;Financial-flexibility motive: Preserve room for future needs.|Assume additional debt creates ₹8 lakh present-value tax benefit but ₹10 expected distress and related costs.~~Net estimated financing effect = ₹8 − ₹10 = −₹2.;;Under a trade-off assessment, that increment is unattractive.;;A firm with sufficient retained cash may also avoid new security issuance under a pecking-order explanation.~~Different theories can predict low additional borrowing for different reasons.~~The estimates are uncertain and do not establish a universal optimal leverage point.|Identify the mechanism behind funding choices.;;Estimate benefits and costs consistently.;;Include information, constraints and future flexibility.
T13.16|Financial distress costs include direct restructuring expenses and indirect damage such as lost customers, disrupted suppliers and forgone investment. Higher leverage increases fixed obligations and can reduce flexibility, especially when cash flows are volatile.~~Optimal leverage depends on business risk, collateral, tax benefits, funding access and stakeholder behaviour. A ratio that appears safe in normal conditions can be fragile when earnings and liquidity deteriorate together.|Direct distress costs: Legal, advisory and restructuring expenses.;;Indirect costs: Operational and relationship damage.;;Expected distress burden: Probability and severity assessed together.|Assume one financing plan has a 5% distress probability with ₹40 lakh cost; another has 15% with ₹60 cost.~~First expected cost = 5% × ₹40 = ₹2.;;Second expected cost = 15% × ₹60 = ₹9.;;Compare the ₹7 incremental expected burden with incremental financing benefits and flexibility effects.~~Higher leverage can increase both the likelihood and severity of distress.~~Simple expected cost does not fully capture liquidity deadlines or stakeholder losses.|Stress cash generation and refinancing.;;Include indirect effects.;;Avoid treating one peer ratio as a universal optimum.
T13.17|Adjusted present value separates operating-project value from financing-related effects. APV = unlevered operating value + financing benefits − financing costs under a consistent decomposition. It can be useful when debt changes materially over the forecast.~~Discount each component according to an appropriate risk and timing assumption. Do not include a tax shield in both operating discounting and a separate financing add-on.|Unlevered operating value: Project value without the chosen debt effects.;;Financing benefit: Tax shields or other justified subsidies.;;Financing cost: Issuance, distress or other incremental funding effects.|Assume unlevered operating value ₹100 lakh, tax-shield PV ₹8 and financing-cost PV ₹3.~~Start with ₹100 operating value.;;Add ₹8 separately valued benefits.;;Subtract ₹3 costs: APV = ₹105.~~Financing adds a net ₹5 under the stated estimates.~~The decomposition makes assumptions visible but does not remove uncertainty in the operating forecast or tax-shield risk.|Value operating flows consistently.;;Model changing debt and tax benefits.;;Check for overlapping financing adjustments.
T13.18|A project-specific discount rate reflects the project's operating risk rather than automatically copying the sponsor's company rate. Changing leverage also changes equity risk, funding costs and sometimes tax-shield treatment. A single constant WACC can be a poor approximation when the financing path changes substantially.~~Comparable operating betas, explicit period-specific rates or APV can help, depending on the model. Financing capacity and failure risk should also be reflected in scenarios rather than hidden in one arbitrary premium.|Corporate average rate: Aggregate existing-business risk.;;Project-specific rate: Risk matched to the proposed investment.;;Changing-leverage approach: Explicit funding path or separate financing valuation.|A mature utility sponsor with 8% WACC considers a risky new technology project. Assume a defensible project-specific operating rate is 14%.~~A ₹20 lakh one-year operating receipt has PV ₹18.52 at 8%.;;At 14%, its PV is ₹17.54.;;Compare the project's cost using the rate matched to its own risk.~~Using the sponsor's lower rate can overvalue a riskier project.~~The 14% is an illustrative assumption; estimate it from relevant business exposures and coherent financing inputs.|Match the rate to project risk.;;Model material leverage changes.;;Cross-check with scenarios and financing constraints.
